  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWe round out the extended Labor Day weekend with a continuation\nof dry and cool Fall-like weather. Stacked low pressure which\nhad been over New England, is drifting away through the Canadian\nMaritime Provinces, leaving high pressure to settle over our\nregion today. Shallow patchy fog can be found in some valleys\nearly this morning, thanks to radiational cooling offered by the\nclear sky. With even less moisture than the already quite dry \natmosphere of the last couple days, just a few tufts of diurnal \ncumulus are anticipated today in otherwise full sunshine with \nhighs of lower-mid 70s after starting off chilly in the mid \n40s-lower 50s at dawn. A very weak wave embedded in northwest \nflow aloft will send scattered high thin clouds into the area \ntonight, yet still enough radiational cooling of the dry air to \nget temperatures into the upper 40s-mid 50s for lows.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nTemperatures will trend warmer Tuesday into Wednesday, as\ninitial ridging is then followed by warm air advection well\nahead of an approaching front which will bring eventual rain.\nHighs top off in upper 70s-lower 80s by Wednesday, yet with\ndewpoints getting well into the 60s and thus a bit of mugginess\nby then. \n\nIn the big picture, low pressure is projected to track from the\nNorthern Plains into Canada during midweek. A cold front will \ntrail distantly from that low, moving through our region around \nWednesday night into Thursday. The ridge begins to flatten later\nWednesday, with a ribbon of higher precipitable water values \npooling with the approaching front. Shortwaves may run out ahead\nof the surface front as well, so chances of showers will begin \nin Central NY Wednesday afternoon before spreading across the \nTwin Tiers and then Wyoming Valley- Poconos Wednesday night. \nModels continue to differ for whether the front pauses its \nprogress briefly late Wednesday night-early Thursday, before a \nbetter-defined upper wave finishes sending it through later \nThursday. Thus while highest likelihood for rain is Wednesday \nnight with the anticipated front, at least a chance of showers \nwill continue Thursday for at least the Twin Tiers-I88 corridor \nand especially Wyoming Valley-Poconos- Catskills. \n\nTiming of front, and limited nature of instability, still keeps \nstronger convection chances on the lower side though there could\nbe some embedded thunder. However, the higher precipitable \nwater environment could yield a pretty good batch of rain. \nCurrent model ensembles show 50-75 percent odds of more than \nhalf an inch for the NY Thruway counties closer to forced ascent\nof jet support as the front settles through, and even nearly 50\npercent probability of an inch toward the Tug Hill area. Finger\nLakes-Twin Tiers southward may not get at much as farther \nnorth, but rain is still expected, with a few to several tenths \nof an inch a reasonable expectation during the Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday timeframe. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nDespite uncertainty for how long the frontal zone lingers into\nThursday, there is solid confidence for dry high pressure to \npay a visit to end the week. However, the generally zonal flow \naloft will keep features moving along, so by Saturday we will\nalready enter a warm air advection regime in the return flow\nbehind the departing high.",
